# ---------------- ledger ----------------
L.append({"chapter": "BTWO-C10", "paragraphs_total": 26, "all_paragraphs_read": True,
          "summary": "Ralph and the Lady of Abundance leave the thick woods and enter a deserted country of steep grassy sandstone valleys, part of the old Land of the Tower. She brings him to a cave above a stream (the 'Chamber of Love'), where she had stayed on her way to the Castle of Abundance. While Ralph hunts and bathes, the Knight of the Sun arrives on Silverfax and slays the Lady; Ralph shoots him dead through the eye. Next day Ralph entombs her in the cave under boughs and stones, raises a cairn over the Knight, and turns Silverfax loose.",
          "geography_notes": "Desert country of steep little sandstone valleys and cliffs beyond the thick woods, once a populous land with castles, now deserted with house-mound tofts; it is part of the Land of the Tower. A little stream beneath cliffs flows toward the Swelling Flood (the 'big river' of the wide valley Ralph crossed with Roger). The desert-house: cave in a sheer cliff some fifty paces beyond the stream, fronted by a greensward table with three thorn bushes, ten feet above the stream, with stepping-stones across a shallow, a deep pool below, and a scree at one end. The sun westers behind them as they travel (they go roughly eastward in the afternoon). The Swelling Flood is to be crossed 'to-morrow'.",
          "open_questions": ["Exact direction of travel from the Castle of Abundance to the cave (sun 'westering behind them' implies eastward-ish)",
                             "How the Knight of the Sun tracked them to the cave"]})
L.append({"chapter": "BTWO-C11", "paragraphs_total": 7, "all_paragraphs_read": True,
          "summary": "At midmorning Ralph leaves the grave alone and on foot, resolving to seek the Well at the World's End. He follows the stream up toward the Swelling Flood, leaves it where it bends northward, and passes into open sheep down country where he finds a road. He meets Companions, two country women with an ass, merchants with servants, and a lord's messenger, then from a high hill-brow sees the Swelling Flood with a wide shallow highway ford, a thorp on the far side, and a peopled valley.",
          "geography_notes": "Sequence: cave/stream -> end of woods -> lower, longer grassy sheep downs -> a road running athwart the downs -> a long high hill whose brow overlooks the Swelling Flood. The stream turns and winds to the northward. The river at the road is very wide and shallow: the ford of a highway, road white on the far bank, a little thorp on the other side, carts and sheds on the hither side, meadows with sheep and neat. It is the same wide valley Ralph crossed with Roger.",
          "open_questions": ["Is the stream Ralph follows 'up' or down? Text says 'followed up the stream' yet the stream flows toward the Swelling Flood, which he is approaching."]})
L.append({"chapter": "BTWO-C12", "paragraphs_total": 14, "all_paragraphs_read": True,
          "summary": "A company of some two score (merchants, their lads, about ten men-at-arms, sumpter beasts) fords the Swelling Flood and comes up the hill; with them ride Ralph's eldest brother Blaise, now a prosperous merchant, and Richard the Red. Ralph joins them on a borrowed horse; Blaise tells of buying booty cheap from Companions and trading town to town. They reach Whitwall by evening before the gates shut and lodge at the hostel of the chapmen.",
          "geography_notes": "The company comes through the thorp and over the ford, then up the hill toward Ralph; crossing the water would be 'the backward way' for Ralph. Whitwall lies on the road on Ralph's side of the Swelling Flood, reachable from the hill-brow above the ford in the rest of an afternoon's ride (arrive 'even'). Blaise's company came to Whitwall through 'the wildwoods'. Whitwall: fair, great, well defensible town with high new walls and gates that are shut at night.",
          "open_questions": ["Direction from the ford to Whitwall is not stated"]})

